## Step-by-Step Implementation

### 1. Prepare Target Application Servers
Jenkins SSH agents require a compatible Java Runtime Environment (JRE/JDK) and an existing remote root directory owned by the SSH user on each target host. From `jumphost`, provision the directories and install `java-17-openjdk-headless` across all three nodes:

```bash
for host in tony@stapp01:Ir0nM@n steve@stapp02:Am3ric@ banner@stapp03:BigGr33n; do
  user_host="${host%%:*}"
  pass="${host#*:}"
  user="${user_host%@*}"
  sshpass -p "$pass" ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no "$user_host" \
    "mkdir -p /home/$user/jenkins && echo '$pass' | sudo -S yum install -y java-17-openjdk-headless"
done
2. Install the SSH Build Agents Plugin
Log into the Jenkins web UI (http://jenkins:8080) using admin / Adm!n321.

Navigate to Manage Jenkins > Plugins > Available plugins.

Search for SSH Build Agents (ssh-slaves), select it, and click Install.

Select Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running, then refresh the browser once the service restarts.

3. Configure Permanent SSH Agent Nodes
Navigate to Manage Jenkins > Nodes > New Node and configure each agent as a Permanent Agent:

